    title: "Golden Ratio",
    hook: "The golden ratio, (1+√5)/2, is a perfectly ordinary algebraic number with an extraordinary press agent. It lives honestly in pentagons, Fibonacci limits, and some phyllotaxis, and dishonestly in a thousand claims about beauty.",
    claim: "φ satisfies φ = 1 + 1/φ and is the most irrational of irrationals, in the sense of continued-fraction approximation. The Fibonacci ratios approach it. [[number-theory|Number theory]] is at home; [[aesthetics|aesthetics]] is often trespassing.",
    scene: "A pentagram in a Greek vase; a sunflower's florets; a PowerPoint asserting that the Parthenon, the pyramids, and a celebrity's face were designed to φ. Two of these scenes are mathematics. One is a marketing department.",
    origin: "Euclid's division in extreme and mean ratio, Pacioli's Divine Proportion with Leonardo's drawings, Kepler's snowflakes, Markowsky's debunking, Livio's popular history. [[combinatorics|Combinatorics]] meets it in tilings and the Fibonacci word. [[fractals|Fractals]] meet it in some self-similar constructions.",
    work: "The honest literature is in algebraic number theory, quasicrystals, and phyllotaxis models (Vogel, Douady–Couder). The dishonest literature is larger.",
    quarrel: "How much of the 'golden' in art is measured, how much superimposed with a flexible ruler? Psychophysics of preference for φ-rectangles is at best weak. The number does not need the myth.",
    still: "A node can earn its keep by being a place where mathematics and wishfulness must be separated. The atlas keeps φ because the wishfulness is part of the culture of number.",
