    id: "cellular-automata",
    title: "Cellular Automata",
    hook: "Cellular automata are worlds of cells that update by local rules, capable of chaos, crystals, and universal computation. Conway's Game of Life is the celebrity; Wolfram's classes are the attempted taxonomy; the idea is older than both.",
    claim: "A grid, a neighborhood, a finite table of next states. From this, gliders, still lives, and — in some rules — a Turing machine. [[self-organization|Self-organization]] without a self; [[emergence|emergence]] as something you can screenshot. [[computation-theory|Computation theory]] is the ceiling: some CA are universal, most are not interesting, a few are inexhaustible. [[complexity|Complexity]] found a toy that was not a toy.",
    scene: "A Life acorn taking 5206 generations to stabilize; a rule 110 demonstration that a one-dimensional trifle can compute; a traffic model that is a CA in denial.",
    origin: "Ulam and von Neumann's self-reproducing machines, Conway 1970, Wolfram's 1980s classification, Langton's lambda, the later physical implementations in materials and chemistry.",
    work: "Gardner's Scientific American columns, Wolfram's A New Kind of Science (a book that is both a hoard and a provocation), the constructive-Life literature of Golly and the speed-of-light engineers.",
    quarrel: "Is the universe a CA? Almost certainly not in the slogan sense. Are Wolfram's four classes a taxonomy or a mood board? The universality results are theorems; the metaphysics is optional.",
