    id: "holism",
    title: "Holism",
    hook: "Holism is the claim that some wholes are not exhausted by their parts, and that analysis can miss the thing. It is [[emergence|emergence]] as a philosophy, [[systems-thinking|systems thinking]] as a loyalty, and a word that has been used both as a method and as a fog.",
    claim: "There are properties of the system that are not properties of the components, or not in the same way. Reduction is powerful and incomplete. [[complexity|Complexity]] is the scientific climate; [[philosophy|philosophy]] of science is the court. Smuts coined the English word in 1926; the habit is ancient.",
    scene: "A melody that is not in the notes taken singly; an organism that dies when 'perfectly' inventoried as chemicals; a team that is worse than its members. Also: a pamphlet that says 'holistic' instead of showing the coupling.",
    origin: "Aristotle's whole-over-parts, Gestalt psychology, Smuts, Bertalanffy's general systems, the anti-reductionist wing of biology. Physics, meanwhile, kept reducing with spectacular success, which is why the claim has to be careful.",
    work: "Nagel on emergence, Anderson's More Is Different, Wimsatt on levels, the systems-biology bet. The honest literature specifies the whole, the parts, and the relation. The dishonest literature just says 'context.'",
    quarrel: "Is holism a discovery or a failure to finish the analysis? Vitalism was a bad holism. Thermodynamics of the living is a better one. The word attracts mysticism the way picnics attract ants; that is not an argument against wholes.",
    still: "A knowledge atlas is a holist instrument: it bets that the graph of ideas is a thing, not only the nodes. The concept earns its keep when it forces a level of description that the parts would not have written.",
    links: "See [[emergence|emergence]], [[systems-thinking|systems thinking]], [[complexity|complexity]], [[philosophy|philosophy]].",
